// Memory: every value is owned by one name, assignment copies, pointers share, a value is freed at the end of its scope
// How memory behaves in assignment, function calls, collections, structs and loops
// Pointers, ownership and freeing, manual memory, sizes and limits
//
// Raga has one rule for every value: a name holds its own value, and assigning it makes a copy.
// Nothing is shared unless you say so with a pointer (section 7). There is no clone, because assignment is the copy.
// A value is freed when its name goes out of scope (section 6). There is no reference counting and no garbage collector.
// Manual memory (section 9) exists for special cases, and is never needed for ordinary code.


// ============================================================
// 1. EVERY VALUE IS OWNED BY ITS NAME
// ============================================================

// A primitive value has a fixed size and is a plain number or flag.
//   i8..u128, isize, usize, f32, f64, f128, bool, char, ptr<T>
// A non-primitive value can own storage that grows, such as the text of a String or the elements of a List.
//   String, Array, List, Tuple, Set, Map, Optional, Result, Function, and every struct, enum, union and class object
//
// The capital letter of a type name says which one it is (01-basics section 10). Both kinds are values: the name holds
// the value itself, and what a non-primitive value owns (its text, its elements, its fields) belongs to it alone.
// Copying a non-primitive value copies everything it owns, at every level.

const count = 5                           // the name holds 5
const text = "hello"                      // the name holds the text, nobody else can see it
const items = [1, 2, 3]                   // the name holds a List and its three elements


// ============================================================
// 2. ASSIGNMENT: ALWAYS A COPY
// ============================================================

// Assigning a primitive copies it. The two names are independent.
const a = 10
let b = a                                 // b is its own copy of 10
b = 20                                    // a is still 10

// Assigning a non-primitive value copies it too, with everything inside it. The two names are independent.
const left = [1, 2, 3]
let right = left                          // right is its own List
right.push(4)                             // left is still 1, 2, 3

// A struct is copied field by field, and a field that holds a List is copied with its elements.
const Box = struct {
    i32 size
    List<i32> items
}
const box1 = Box(size = 1, items = [1, 2])
let box2 = box1                           // a copy: box2 and box1 are two Boxes
box2.size = 9                             // box1.size is still 1
box2.items.push(3)                        // box1.items is still 1, 2

// A copy goes all the way down, so a List of Lists is copied with every inner List.
const nested = [[1], [2]]
let nested_copy = nested
nested_copy.get(0).push(9)                // nested is unchanged, the inner Lists were copied too

// Rebinding a name never changes the value another name holds.
let list1 = [1]
const list2 = list1
list1 = [2]                               // list1 holds a new List, list2 still holds 1

// A copy with some fields changed is a copy, then the change. There is no clone.
let box3 = box1
box3.size = 5                             // Box(size = 5, items = [1, 2]), box1 is unchanged

// == does not compare Lists, structs or other non-primitive values, see 01-basics section 7.
// Compare the parts you care about: left.len() == right.len() - 1.


// ============================================================
// 3. SHARING: A POINTER TO A VARIABLE
// ============================================================

// To make two names work on one value, take a pointer to the variable. addr(x) gives a ptr<T>, and p.* is the value.
// The pointer is not a copy: a change through it is a change of the variable (section 7 has the full rules).
let shared = [1, 2, 3]
ptr<List<i32>> alias = addr(shared)       // alias points at the variable shared
alias.*.push(4)                           // shared is 1, 2, 3, 4
const alias_len = alias.*.len()           // 4

// A function that needs to change its caller's value takes a pointer (section 5).
// A function that only reads can take the value itself, and the copy costs nothing you can see.

// String is immutable, so a String copy can never be told apart from the original. It is still a copy.
const name1 = "ann"
const name2 = name1
const name3 = name1.upper()               // upper makes a new String, name1 is unchanged


// ============================================================
// 4. CONST, LET AND MUTABLE VALUES
// ============================================================

// const protects the whole value. The name cannot be rebound, a primitive cannot change, and a non-primitive value
// cannot be changed in place: no field can be assigned and no method that changes it can be called.
const fixed_list = [1, 2]
const fixed_first = fixed_list.get(0)     // reading is fine
// fixed_list.push(3)                  // ERROR: fixed_list is const, push changes it
// fixed_list = [9]                    // ERROR: a const name cannot be rebound

const fixed_box = Box(size = 1, items = List<i32>())
// fixed_box.size = 2                  // ERROR: fixed_box is const, a field cannot be assigned
// fixed_box.items.push(1)             // ERROR: fixed_box is const, its items cannot change
let thawed = fixed_box                    // a copy is a new value, and let makes it changeable
thawed.size = 2

// What can change in place, on a let name:
//   String   nothing, it is immutable
//   Array    an element can be replaced with set, the size is fixed
//   Tuple    a slot can be replaced with set, the size is fixed
//   List     push, pop, shift, unshift, set
//   Set      add, remove
//   Map      set, remove
//   struct   its fields, and a method of its own that assigns to this

// To keep a value from changing, declare it const. A const value can still be copied into a let name.


// ============================================================
// 5. FUNCTIONS AND MEMORY
// ============================================================

// Arguments follow the one rule: every parameter is a copy of what the caller passed.
// A function that changes its parameter changes only its own copy.
const add_ten_copy = function(List<i32> items) List<i32> {
    let local = items
    local.push(10)
    return(local)                         // the caller gets this List back as a value
}
let numbers = [1, 2]
const bigger = add_ten_copy(numbers)      // bigger is 1, 2, 10 and numbers is still 1, 2

// To change the caller's value, take a pointer. The caller writes addr to say so, so a change is never a surprise.
const add_ten = function(ptr<List<i32>> items) {
    items.*.push(10)
}
add_ten(addr(numbers))                    // numbers is now 1, 2, 10

const bumped = function(i32 n) i32 {
    let local = n                         // n is a copy of the caller's number
    local = local + 1
    return(local)
}
let level = 1
const next = bumped(level)                // 2, level is still 1

// A function returns a value: the caller gets its own value, and a copy is made only when it cannot be avoided.
// A value made inside a function is the caller's after the call.
const make_list = function() List<i32> {
    const made = [1, 2, 3]
    return(made)
}
const kept = make_list()

// A default parameter value is made again on every call, so calls never share it.
const collect = function(i32 item, List<i32> into = List<i32>()) List<i32> {
    let result = into
    result.push(item)
    return(result)
}
const c1 = collect(1)                     // 1
const c2 = collect(2)                     // 2, not 1, 2
// A struct field default is worked out again for every value, like a parameter default.
const b1 = Box(size = 0, items = List<i32>())
let b2 = Box(size = 0, items = List<i32>())
b2.items.push(1)                          // b1.items is still empty


// ============================================================
// 6. LIFETIME: A VALUE IS FREED WHEN ITS NAME GOES OUT OF SCOPE
// ============================================================

// A value lives until the end of the block its name was declared in. Then it is freed, with everything it owns.
{
    const short = 1
    const also_short = Box(size = 2, items = List<i32>())
}
// short and also_short are gone here, and the Box and its List are freed at the closing brace.

// Rebinding a name frees the value it held, right away.
let holder = [1, 2, 3]                    // the List lives in holder
holder = [4]                              // the first List is freed, holder holds the new one

// A value is owned by exactly one place, so it is freed exactly once. A member of a collection is owned by the collection
// and is freed with it. Removing it hands it to you:
let owner = [[1], [2]]
const taken = owner.pop()                 // taken owns the inner List now, owner no longer does

// A copy is a new owner. Copying into another variable never keeps the first value alive.
let outer_copy = List<i32>()
{
    const inner_value = [1, 2]
    outer_copy = inner_value              // outer_copy holds its own copy, which outlives the block
}
const survivor = outer_copy.len()         // still valid: 2

// Temporary values are freed at the end of the statement that created them.
const length = [1, 2, 3].len()            // the List lived only for this statement

// A loop name is a new name on every pass and is gone at the end of each pass.
for (range(0, 3)) |pass| {
    const made = [pass]                   // created and freed on every pass
}

// A destructor of a class (07-classes-and-traits) runs at the point where its object is freed, which is the closing brace.

// A function declared inside a block uses the variables around it (a closure, 04-functions section 15). It uses the
// variables themselves, it does not copy them, so a change inside the function is seen outside and the other way round.
// That is why a closure cannot outlive the variables it uses:
//   - A closure that is only called while the variables are alive is always fine: callbacks, blocks, nested helpers.
//   - Returning a closure that uses a local variable of the function that returns it is a compile error.
//   - Storing it somewhere that outlives the block is not checked, like a pointer (section 7).
// A closure that must keep state keeps it in a struct, or in memory from alloc (section 9):
// const bad_counter = function() Function<void, i32> {      // ERROR: next uses count, which is gone when bad_counter ends
//     let count = 0
//     const next = function() i32 { return(count) }
//     return(next)
// }
const Counter = struct {
    i32 total

    const add = function(i32 amount) i32 {
        this.total = this.total + amount
        return(this.total)
    }
}
let my_total = Counter(total = 0)
const t1 = my_total.add(5)                // 5
const t2 = my_total.add(7)                // 12


// ============================================================
// 7. POINTERS
// ============================================================

// A pointer holds the address of a variable. Its type is ptr<T>, with the type of what it points to in angle brackets,
// like every type argument. ptr is a primitive type, so its name is lowercase and copying a pointer copies the address.
// The two operations are:
//   addr(variable)   the address of a variable, a ptr<T>   (a function, like sizeof and alloc)
//   pointer.*        the value the pointer points to, a T  (postfix .*)
// The dereference is written after the pointer, like a field access, so it chains left to right
// and cannot be mistaken for a multiplication: p.* reads "the value of p". & stays the bitwise and, and only that.
i32 target = 7
ptr<i32> p = addr(target)                 // explicit pointer type
const q = addr(target)                    // inferred: ptr<i32>
const seven = p.*                         // 7
p.* = 8                                   // target is now 8, p.* can be assigned like a variable
p.* += 1                                  // target is now 9, the compound operators work too
const nine = q.*                          // 9, q points to the same variable
// p.* = 2.5                              // ERROR: the pointer is a ptr<i32>, 2.5 is an f64
// ptr<f64> bad = addr(target)            // ERROR: addr(target) is a ptr<i32>
// const bad = addr(1 + 2)                // ERROR: only a variable has an address, not a temporary value

// A pointer must be initialized like any variable. null is the empty pointer, like C's NULL.
// It can be given to any ptr<T>, and only to a ptr<T>: null is not a value of any other type.
ptr<i32> empty = null
const is_empty = empty == null            // true
// i32 count = null                       // ERROR: only a ptr<T> can be null, use Optional<T> for a value that may be absent
// const bad = null                       // ERROR: null has no type of its own, write ptr<i32> p = null
// empty.*                                // ERROR at run time: a null pointer cannot be dereferenced, it throws an Error (06-errors)

// == and != on pointers compare addresses, like == on a primitive compares the number.
const same = p == q                       // true: both hold the address of target
i32 other = 9
const different = p == addr(other)        // false: another variable, even with the same value

// A pointer to a variable that holds a struct points at that variable. A field is reached with .* like any value,
// there is no shortcut, so a dereference always shows where memory is followed.
const Spot = struct {
    f64 x
    f64 y
}
let spot = Spot(x = 1.0, y = 2.0)
const sp = addr(spot)                     // ptr<Spot>
sp.*.x = 5.0                              // changes spot.x
const sx = sp.*.x                         // 5.0
sp.* = Spot(x = 9.0, y = 0.0)             // spot now holds a new Spot
let spot_copy = sp.*                      // a copy: changing spot_copy leaves spot alone
// const bad = sp.x                       // ERROR: sp is a pointer, write sp.*.x
// sp points at the variable spot. To give C the address of the fields of the Spot, use spot.raw():
// every struct and every untagged union has the layout of a C struct (section 10), and two methods are generated for it:
//   raw()            a ptr<u8>, C's void *: the address of the fields, valid while the struct is alive
//   Type.from_raw(p) a new struct made by copying the fields from such an address
const raw_spot = spot.raw()               // ptr<u8>: where the fields of spot are, in C layout, to give to a C function
const from_c = Spot.from_raw(raw_spot)    // a new Spot copied from those bytes, a separate value
// A C function that takes a struct by value gets the fields copied, as the C calling convention says.
// raw() is not addr(spot): addr(spot) is a pointer to the variable spot, raw() is the address of the fields.

// A function that takes a pointer can change a variable of its caller, which a plain parameter cannot (section 5).
const increment = function(ptr<i32> counter) {
    counter.* = counter.* + 1
}
let hits = 0
increment(addr(hits))
increment(addr(hits))                     // hits is 2

// A pointer can point to a pointer. Each .* peels off one level.
ptr<ptr<i32>> pp = addr(p)
const via_two = pp.*.*                    // 9, the value of target

// A pointer to a variable that holds a List points at the variable. The List is the value of the variable.
let words = ["a"]
ptr<List<String>> wp = addr(words)
wp.* = ["b"]                              // words now holds a new List
wp.*.push("c")                            // .* gives the List itself, which is then changed in place

// A pointer into a collection: at(i) gives the pointer to element i of a List or an Array.
// get(i) gives a copy of the element, at(i) gives the element itself. The pointer is valid until the collection
// grows or shrinks, as a push can move its elements.
let scores = [10, 20, 30]
const second = scores.get(1)              // a copy: 20
ptr<i32> second_ref = scores.at(1)
second_ref.* = 25                         // scores is 10, 25, 30
scores.at(2).* += 5                       // scores is 10, 25, 35

// Rules:
//   - There is no pointer arithmetic and no indexing through a pointer: p + 1 and p[0] are errors.
//   - A pointer to a const variable cannot be made: addr(const_name) is an error, because the value must not change.
//   - A pointer does not own anything and does not keep anything alive. The variable it points to owns the value.
//   - A pointer can be converted only by an explicit cast, like every other conversion:
//       ptr<u8>(p)      from a ptr<i32> to a ptr<u8>, which is C's void *
//       usize(p)        the address as a number
//       ptr<i32>(n)     a number as an address, never checked
// const bad = p + 1                      // ERROR: no pointer arithmetic
// const fixed = 5
// const bad = addr(fixed)                // ERROR: fixed is const

// A pointer to a variable is only valid while that variable is alive. After its block ends the variable is gone
// and the pointer dangles. Following Raga's rule for C, using a dangling pointer is undefined, it is not checked.
// The obvious case is rejected at compile time:
// const leak = function() ptr<i32> {
//     i32 local = 1
//     return(addr(local))                 // ERROR: the address of a local variable cannot be returned
// }
// Storing the address somewhere that outlives the block is not checked and is your responsibility:
ptr<i32> outer = null
{
    i32 inner = 5
    outer = addr(inner)                   // outer now holds an address that is valid only inside this block
}
// outer.*                                // undefined: inner is gone

// C types: a C pointer is a ptr<T> of the matching type, and a void * is a ptr<u8>.
//   int *          ptr<i32>        char *         ptr<i8>        void *         ptr<u8>

// A pointer is also how a type is used polymorphically: a ptr<Base> can point at a variable of a class that extends
// Base, and a call through it chooses the method of the real class. A class object assigned to a variable of its base
// class would lose the extra fields, so that is an error (07-classes-and-traits section 9).


// ============================================================
// 8. OWNERSHIP AND FREEING
// ============================================================

// Because a value has exactly one owner, freeing is simple and happens at a point you can read in the code:
//   - a name that goes out of scope frees its value,
//   - a name that is rebound frees the value it held,
//   - a collection that is freed frees its elements, a struct that is freed frees its fields,
//   - a temporary is freed at the end of its statement.
// Nothing counts references and nothing runs in the background. The program never pauses to clean up.

// Values cannot form a cycle, because no value holds another one: a copy holds its own copy. A pointer can point
// back at its owner, and it does not own, so it keeps nothing alive and nothing is leaked:
const Node = struct {
    i32 id
    ptr<Node> parent                      // points back, does not own: ptr<T> is a primitive
}
let root = Node(id = 1, parent = null)
const child = Node(id = 2, parent = addr(root))
const parent_id = child.parent.*.id       // 1: valid while root is alive

// A pointer must not be used after the variable it points to is freed. That is the one thing to look after:
// keep the owner in a block that is at least as large as the block of every pointer to it.

// A type can free more than memory: a class with a destructor (07-classes-and-traits) closes a file or a connection
// at the same point, the closing brace of its scope.


// ============================================================
// 9. MANUAL MEMORY
// ============================================================

// Almost all code never needs this: lists, strings and the rest manage themselves. Manual memory is for
// the rare case that needs memory whose lifetime is not tied to a block, such as a buffer shared with C.
// It is made of three functions, and the memory it gives is never freed by Raga:
//   alloc<T>(count)   allocates room for count values of the primitive type T, filled with zeros, and gives a ptr<T>
//   p.at(i)           the pointer to element i of what p points to, without any check of the position
//   free(p)           gives the memory back
// T must be a primitive type: non-primitive values already manage themselves.
// There is no pointer arithmetic, p.at(i) is how you reach the other elements.

const buffer = alloc<u8>(4)               // four zeroed bytes, a ptr<u8>
buffer.at(0).* = 65
buffer.at(1).* = 66
const second_byte = buffer.at(1).*        // 66
free(buffer)                              // the memory is released

// One primitive value, on the memory that is managed by hand.
const manual = alloc<i64>()               // count defaults to 1, a ptr<i64> that holds 0
manual.* = 3
free(manual)

// alloc fails by throwing an Error when there is no memory left, see 06-errors.
// Use try / finally so the memory is freed on every path:
const block = alloc<i32>(100)
try {
    block.at(0).* = 1
} finally {
    free(block)
}

// Rules:
//   - Memory from alloc is freed only by free, exactly once. If it is never freed it is a leak.
//   - free(null) does nothing, like in C.
//   - Freeing twice, or using a pointer after free, is undefined. Nothing is checked, as for any dangling pointer.
//   - Only memory from alloc can be freed. free(addr(x)) of a variable is an error, a pointer from addr is not allocated.
//   - p.at(i) does not check i, going past the end is undefined, like indexing past the end in C.
//   - alloc and free are not for non-primitive types: alloc<List<i32>>() is an error.
// free(addr(target))                     // ERROR: target is a variable, it was not allocated
// const bad = alloc<List<i32>>()         // ERROR: a List manages itself


// ============================================================
// 10. SIZES AND LIMITS
// ============================================================

// sizeof<T>() gives the size of a type in bytes as a usize, and alignof<T>() its alignment.
// A primitive has a fixed size known at compile time:
//   i8 1, i16 2, i32 4, i64 8, i128 16, f32 4, f64 8, bool 1, char 4, isize and usize and ptr<T> as wide as a pointer
//   an enum is the size of an i32
//   a struct follows the C layout: the fields in the order they are written, each at a multiple of its alignment,
//   and the whole struct padded to a multiple of its largest alignment
//   a tagged union is as large as its biggest variant plus the tag that says which variant it holds
// A struct, an enum or a union is stored in the variable that holds it, so sizeof is the size of that variable.
// A field that holds a String or a collection counts as one handle as wide as a pointer: what it owns grows, so it is
// not part of the size. Every struct and untagged union has this C layout, so raw() can give C its address.
// String and the collections grow, so they have no fixed size and no sizeof.
const size_of_i32 = sizeof<i32>()           // 4
const size_of_spot = sizeof<Spot>()         // 16: two f64

// Padding: the fields are never reordered, so their order changes the size.
const Loose = struct {
    u8 flag
    i32 count
    u8 mark
}
const Tight = struct {
    i32 count
    u8 flag
    u8 mark
}
const loose_size = sizeof<Loose>()         // 12: 1 + 3 padding + 4 + 1 + 3 padding
const tight_size = sizeof<Tight>()         // 8: 4 + 1 + 1 + 2 padding
const loose_align = alignof<Loose>()       // 4

// Limits:
//   - The stack has a fixed size, set by the system (usually a few megabytes). A function that calls itself
//     without ever stopping overflows it, and the program ends with an error message. That cannot be caught.
//     Values are stored in their variables, so a very large struct in a local variable uses the stack too.
//   - The heap is limited by the memory of the machine. Running out of it, in alloc or when a collection grows,
//     throws an Error that can be caught, see 06-errors.
//   - The integer types have fixed sizes, so a value that does not fit is truncated by a cast and never grows,
//     see 01-basics.
// const forever = function(i32 n) i32 { return(forever(n + 1)) }   // runs until the stack is full


// ============================================================
// 11. A VIEW INTO AN ARRAY: A SLICE STRUCT
// ============================================================

// A slice is a part of an Array or a List that is looked at in place, without copying it. It is not a built-in type:
// it is a struct that holds a pointer to the Array and the part it shows. A plain Array field would be a copy,
// so the slice holds a pointer, and the slice and the Array always agree.
const Slice = struct {
    ptr<Array<i32>> source
    usize start
    usize length

    const get = function(usize index) i32 {
        return(this.source.*.get(this.start + index))
    }
    const len = function() usize {
        return(this.length)
    }
}

let samples = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50}
const middle = Slice(source = addr(samples), start = 1, length = 3)     // 20, 30, 40
const middle_first = middle.get(0)                 // 20
const middle_len = middle.len()                    // 3
samples.set(1, 99)
const middle_changed = middle.get(0)               // 99: the slice points at the Array, it holds no copy of the samples

// Rules:
//   - A slice is a struct, so it follows every rule of a struct. It does not own the Array: samples must outlive the slice.
//   - To get a separate part instead, copy the samples into a new Array or List: the slice and the copy then do not agree.
//   - Nothing checks the part against the Array: get with an index outside the part reads what the Array has there, or throws
//     when the Array has no such position.


// ============================================================
// 12. COMMON MISTAKES
// ============================================================

// Each mistake is shown with what happens, and with the way that does what was meant.

// 1. Expecting two names to see the same change. Assignment copies, so use a pointer to work on one value.
let cm_items = [1, 2]
let cm_backup = cm_items                          // a copy, which is a backup
ptr<List<i32>> cm_live = addr(cm_items)           // the same List, through a pointer
cm_items.push(3)
const cm_backup_len = cm_backup.len()             // 2: the copy did not change
const cm_live_len = cm_live.*.len()               // 3: the pointer sees the variable

// 2. Changing a List while looping over it. It throws an Error (03-control-flow section 10), so loop over a copy.
let cm_queue = [1, 2, 3]
const cm_snapshot = cm_queue
for (cm_snapshot) |cm_n| {
    if (cm_n == 2) { cm_queue.push(9) }           // fine: the loop goes over the copy, not over cm_queue
}
// for (cm_queue) |cm_n| { cm_queue.push(cm_n) }     // throws an Error: the List changes while the loop goes over it

// 3. Expecting a function to change its argument. The parameter is a copy: pass a pointer to change the caller's value.
const cm_fill = function(ptr<List<i32>> target_list) {
    target_list.*.push(0)
}
cm_fill(addr(cm_queue))                           // cm_queue got a 0

// 4. Comparing Lists, structs or other values with ==. It is not defined for them. Compare the parts you care about.
const cm_left = [1]
const cm_right = [1]
const cm_parts_equal = cm_left.get(0) == cm_right.get(0)    // true: compare the parts yourself
// const cm_equal = cm_left == cm_right           // ERROR: == is not defined for List<i32>

// 5. Writing sp.x on a pointer. A pointer has no fields: follow it first with .*, as in sp.*.x, see section 7.

// 6. Keeping a pointer to a variable that has gone out of scope, see section 7.
//      returning addr(local) from a function is a compile error, and a pointer stored elsewhere is not checked.
// 7. Expecting an assignment through a pointer to a List variable to change that List only: p.* = [9] makes the variable hold
//      a new List, and the old List is freed.

// 8. Forgetting free on memory from alloc, or calling it twice, see section 9. Use try / finally.
const cm_block = alloc<i32>(4)
try {
    cm_block.at(1).* = 7
} finally {
    free(cm_block)                                // once, whatever happened inside the try
}

// 9. Expecting sizeof of a struct to include what its heap fields hold: a field that holds a List or a String is only
//     one handle. The number of elements does not change it.

// 10. Assuming a struct is as small as its fields add up: padding is added, see section 10.
const PaddedPair = struct {
    u8 flag
    u32 count
}
const cm_padded_size = sizeof<PaddedPair>()       // 8, not 5: three bytes of padding come after flag
